  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Assisting Commonwealth Member Countries Conserve and Sustainably Use the Oceans</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project works in partnership with the Climate Finance Access Hub and Natural Resources projects following a whole of directorate approach. With regards to the Climate Finance Access Hub this partnership will be operationalised through the following concrete steps: 1.) a webinar will be organised to presenting the forthcoming blue bonds handbook to all Commonwealth Climate Finance Access Hub Advisers, 2.) we will coordinate with climate finance access hub advisers that are resident in countries where we have ongoing technical assistance projects on BBNJ and Maritime Boundaries, 3.) we will repropose working with the Commonwealth Climate Finance Access Hub on NBSAPs. However, this activitiy will only be possible if financed under another PDD. We will work with natural resources on DSM. The Ocean project consists of three sub-projects. These are the Commonwealth Blue Charter, the Ocean Action Incubator, and Technical Assistance Projects. Commonwealth Blue Charter: The CBC consists of Action Groups each championed by at least one CMC. Additionally, there will be two working groups: the Blue Finance Working Group and the Ocean Youth Working Group. The Ocean project team advises and assists in organising these groups. It also drafts and commissions technical work and trainings materials and organises online training and webinars. The Ocean project team also supports two regional centres of excellence: the Centre of Excellence for Oceanography and the Blue Economy in Antigua and Barbuda and the Blue Charter Centre of Excellence in Cyprus. In line with the SG&#x2019;s call for regional centres this Project will step up support for these regional centres if financing is adequate to do so. Ocean Action Incubator: The Incubator will provide grants for small-sized projects, including training and rapid assessments to governments to support innovative ocean projects. It is a unique model as grants are provided directly to CMCs for early-stage projects; this contrasts with most grant programmes which finance NGOs or communities. These grants have leveraged significant co-finance, of up to 45% of the grant value. It is recognised that given internal financing constraints a second round of the incubator is likely to be dependent on external financing. Technical Assistance Projects: The Ocean project team will provide detailed, confidential, bilateral and regional technical assistance to CMCs on BBNJ implementation and ratification, maritime boundary delimitation , on a request driven basis. We will provide technical assistance in other areas if provided with the financial resources to do so. In undertaking work on the Oceans, the Commonwealth Secretariat has the following comparative advantages: high demand from our member countries, convening power, extensive partnership networks, existing mechanisms, technical expertise, and a track record of success.</narrative>
  </description>
